    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Victim-Owned Websites — Subdomain brute force", "technique": "T1594", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 09:02:11 CEST, Suricata logged a burst of 'ET SCAN Aggressive Web Crawl' and 'Subdomain Enumeration Detected' alerts with data.srcip=45.139.105.44 against data.dstip=10.108.57.177. The source sent ~30 sequential HTTP requests to http://10.108.57.177/ each carrying a different Host header override (www.corp.local, mail.corp.local, vpn.corp.local, portal.corp.local, admin.corp.local, dev.corp.local, staging.corp.local, sso.corp.local, api.corp.local ...). This is DNS-brute-force-via-HTTP: the operator avoids DNS query logging by probing a known web host IP with Host: sub.corp.local headers and reading which subdomains respond distinctly. User-Agent rotation across 5 browser/curl strings simulates organic traffic. Polite ~9s jittered timing keeps it under naive rate thresholds.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET SCAN Aggressive Web Crawl' (rule 31101) plus 'Subdomain Enumeration Detected' (rule 31110) both fired with data.srcip=45.139.105.44 against a single data.dstip, with >25 distinct data.url Host-header values inside a 10-minute window.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Victim-Owned Websites — HTTP/HTTPS dual-stack probing", "technique": "T1594", "what_happened": "At ~09:10 CEST the same data.srcip=45.139.105.44 narrowed to the high-value subdomain candidates discovered in step 1 (admin, internal, intranet, sso, auth, vpn, portal, api, api-prod, api-internal, dev, staging, jira, confluence, gitlab, jenkins, owa, webmail) and probed BOTH http:// and https:// for each. On the HTTPS leg it also performed TLS handshakes to grab certificate CN/SAN data — cert transparency style enumeration to confirm which subdomains are real. Suricata fired 'Web Crawler / Scanner Detected' (rule 31100) repeatedly across the dual-stack sweep.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip continuing from step 1, now generating paired http+https requests to the narrowed subdomain set; rule 31100 'Web Crawler / Scanner Detected' fired with the same data.http.user_agent rotation pool observed in step 1.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:10:33+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Victim-Owned Websites — Cross-subdomain link harvesting & sensitive paths", "technique": "T1594", "what_happened": "At ~09:16 CEST the same data.srcip=45.139.105.44 fetched the main corp.local site pages — /, /login, /portal, /support, /intranet, /sso, /about, /contact — and parsed each HTML body for href/src references to *.corp.local subdomains, expanding its target list from the initial wordlist to whatever the site itself links to. Several requests hit sensitive paths; Suricata fired 'Sensitive Directory Access Attempt' (rule 31120). This is the recursive-crawl step: discovery on one host feeds discovery of many, the scope expands organically from a single seed.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip=45.139.105.44; data.url values now include /login, /portal, /intranet, /sso and the crawl is recursive — links harvested from fetched pages become new targets. Rule 31120 'Sensitive Directory Access Attempt' fired on the /intranet and /sso path hits.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:16:02+0200"},
